   "title": "The active and the passive with ser",
   "body": "<p>Compare “The council approved the plan” and “The plan was approved by the council.” The two sentences say the same thing, but in the first the subject is the one who acts, the agent, and in the second the subject is the thing acted upon, the patient. The first sentence is active, and the second is passive. Spanish says <i lang=\"es\">La asamblea aprobó la propuesta</i> and <i lang=\"es\">La propuesta fue aprobada por la asamblea</i>. In the passive the direct object of the active sentence becomes the subject, the verb is a form of <i lang=\"es\">ser</i> with the participle, the participle agrees with the subject, and the agent, if it is expressed, is introduced by <i lang=\"es\">por</i> (Manual 41.1.2). The verb <i lang=\"es\">ser</i> may stand in any tense and in the infinitive: <i lang=\"es\">Fue elegida</i>, <i lang=\"es\">Ha sido elegida</i>, <i lang=\"es\">Desea ser elegida</i> (“She was elected,” “She has been elected,” “She wishes to be elected”) (Manual 41.2.2).</p><p>The agent is often left out, and the passive is chosen to put the patient first or to keep the agent in the background (Manual 41.1.2). Only transitive verbs with an expressed object form this passive, and not every transitive verb gives a natural one. The Academy notes that the passive is strained with verbs of state, such as <i lang=\"es\">saber</i> and <i lang=\"es\">temer</i>, which have no true agent, and that no passive is formed from a verb whose object is not expressed (Manual 41.2.1). The Academy also recommends avoiding a passive whose subject would be an indirect object, such as those that appear in the Spanish spoken in the United States under the influence of English (Manual 41.2.1). Because in Spanish the passive is built by syntax and not by an ending of the verb, the Academy prefers to speak of passive constructions and not of a passive voice (Manual 41.1.1).</p><p>Latin has a passive voice in the strict sense, because its verb has passive endings: <i lang=\"la\">amō</i> (“I love”) and <i lang=\"la\">amor</i> (“I am loved”) (Manual 41.1.1). The Latin agent is in the ablative with <i lang=\"la\">ā</i> or <i lang=\"la\">ab</i>: <i lang=\"la\">fīlius ā patre vocātur</i>, “the son is called by his father” (Allen and Greenough §275 and §405). Spanish has no such endings, and the passive is a construction of <i lang=\"es\">ser</i> and the participle, with <i lang=\"es\">por</i> for the agent: <i lang=\"es\">El hijo es llamado por su padre</i>.</p>",
